Z||000013||FastFieros||10-25-2001||08:00 AM||||You listed some nice engine choices but not your budget. If it is unlimited and you want the a streetable car with drag race qualities, the 3.8SC seems to do the job. Nice easy install after the wiring harness issue is taken care of. <P>2.7 duke was interesting you mention. Do you really know what it takes to get to a 200HP 4 banger OEM block motor? I do. I built 52 of these motors in 96-98 racing midgets here in North Texas. I have 9 cylinder heads sitting in a trailer that have various porting on various configurations of the 16 different 4 cylinder heads available for the (R) code motor. Here is some of what it takes.<BR>311 casting crank will get you a 3 inch stroke but very heavy duty and can turn 7200 RPM without failures after balancing. 355 crank, have you even heard of this crank? I have one left. It is a Forged crank for a early 70's 4 banger that raced on the NHRA circuit. It will give you 3.25 inch stroke and allow a 6.125 rod to be used on a Holden flat top piston. Deck the head .090 and shave the block to zero deck and you are pushing 14:1 using a 702 cast number head. I could turn this motor 7600 RPM for 31 races with 20 minute features without failures. The one small detail that hurts using this on the street is we used mechcanical fuel injection and it gets about 6 miles to the gallon.<P>3.4 motor, well I have installed a few of these now. Not real impressive, but it sure drops right in the car after the starter holes are resolved.<P>Good luck on whatever choice.<P>Loyde<BR>dallas texas <A HREF="http://www.fastfieros.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fastfieros.com</A> ||||reg||14
